The theorem `definable_iff_extended_polynomial` is universally quantified over an arity $k \in \mathbb{N}$ (with no hypotheses, including $k = 0$) and a function $f : (\mathrm{Fin}\, k \to \mathbb{N}) \to \mathbb{N}$, and asserts the conjunction of three pairwise logical equivalences:
$$\big(\mathrm{IsPiecewisePolynomial}(k, f) \iff \mathrm{IsExtendedPolyInd}(f)\big) \;\land\; \big(\mathrm{IsLambdaDefinable}(k, f) \iff \mathrm{IsExtendedPolyInd}(f)\big) \;\land\; \big(\mathrm{IsLambdaDefinable}(k, f) \iff \mathrm{IsPiecewisePolynomial}(k, f)\big),$$
where the three predicates on $k$ and $f$ are defined as follows:

1. **Piecewise polynomial ($\mathrm{IsPiecewisePolynomial}(k, f)$):** For every Boolean zero-test signature $\sigma : \mathrm{Fin}\, k \to \mathrm{Bool}$, there exists a function $P : (\mathrm{Fin}\, k \to \mathbb{N}) \to \mathbb{N}$ such that $\mathrm{IsPolynomial}(P)$ holds and for every input tuple $v : \mathrm{Fin}\, k \to \mathbb{N}$, if $(v(i) \neq 0) = \sigma(i)$ as Booleans for every $i \in \mathrm{Fin}\, k$, then $f(v) = P(v)$. Here $\mathrm{IsPolynomial}$ is the inductive predicate on functions $(\mathrm{Fin}\, k \to \mathbb{N}) \to \mathbb{N}$ generated by constant functions $v \mapsto c$ ($c \in \mathbb{N}$) and coordinate projections $v \mapsto v(i)$ ($i \in \mathrm{Fin}\, k$), and closed under pointwise addition $v \mapsto P_1(v) + P_2(v)$ and pointwise multiplication $v \mapsto P_1(v) \cdot P_2(v)$.

2. **Inductively defined extended polynomial ($\mathrm{IsExtendedPolyInd}(f)$):** $f$ belongs to the smallest indexed family of sets of functions $(\mathrm{Fin}\, n \to \mathbb{N}) \to \mathbb{N}$ (across all $n \in \mathbb{N}$) closed under the following six rules:
   - `const`: for any $n \in \mathbb{N}$ and $c \in \mathbb{N}$, the constant function $v \mapsto c$ belongs to the class at arity $n$;
   - `proj`: for any $n \in \mathbb{N}$ and $i \in \mathrm{Fin}\, n$, the projection $v \mapsto v(i)$ belongs to the class at arity $n$;
   - `add`: if $g_1, g_2 : (\mathrm{Fin}\, n \to \mathbb{N}) \to \mathbb{N}$ belong to the class, then $v \mapsto g_1(v) + g_2(v)$ belongs to the class;
   - `mul`: if $g_1, g_2 : (\mathrm{Fin}\, n \to \mathbb{N}) \to \mathbb{N}$ belong to the class, then $v \mapsto g_1(v) \cdot g_2(v)$ belongs to the class;
   - `cond`: if $g_1, g_2, g_3 : (\mathrm{Fin}\, n \to \mathbb{N}) \to \mathbb{N}$ belong to the class, then $v \mapsto \mathrm{condNat}(g_1(v), g_2(v), g_3(v))$ belongs to the class, where $\mathrm{condNat}(a, b, i)$ equals $a$ if $i = 0$ and $b$ if $i \neq 0$;
   - `comp`: for any $n, m \in \mathbb{N}$, if $g : (\mathrm{Fin}\, m \to \mathbb{N}) \to \mathbb{N}$ belongs to the class at arity $m$ and $h_j : (\mathrm{Fin}\, n \to \mathbb{N}) \to \mathbb{N}$ belongs to the class at arity $n$ for every $j \in \mathrm{Fin}\, m$, then the composite $v \mapsto g(j \mapsto h_j(v))$ belongs to the class at arity $n$.

3. **$\lambda$-definability ($\mathrm{IsLambdaDefinable}(k, f)$):** There exists a term $t : \mathrm{Term}$ in the simply typed Church-style $\lambda$-calculus such that $\mathrm{DefinesFunction}(k, t, f)$ holds, meaning:
   - $t$ has type $\mathrm{nuArity}(k)$ under the inductive typing relation `HasType` (where variables $x = (\mathrm{id}, \mathrm{ty}) \in \mathbb{N} \times \mathrm{Ty}$ carry their own type, base type is $0 := \mathrm{Ty.zero}$, $\mathrm{endo} := 0 \to 0$, $\nu := (0 \to 0) \to (0 \to 0)$, $\mathrm{nuArity}(0) := \nu$, and $\mathrm{nuArity}(m+1) := \nu \to \mathrm{nuArity}(m)$);
   - $t$ is closed (`IsClosed t`, i.e., `freeVars t = []`, meaning $t$ has no free variable occurrences); and
   - for every $v : \mathrm{Fin}\, k \to \mathbb{N}$, the left-associated application $\mathrm{appMany}(t, \mathrm{numeralArgs}(k, v)) = (\cdots((t\, \overline{v(0)})\, \overline{v(1)})\cdots \overline{v(k-1)})$ reduces via $\twoheadrightarrow_{\beta\alpha}$ (`ReducesTo`) to $\overline{f(v)}$, where $\overline{n} := \mathrm{churchNumeral}(n) := \lambda \alpha_1.\, \lambda x_0.\, \mathrm{iterApp}(\alpha_1, x_0, n)$ with $\alpha_1 = (1, \mathrm{endo})$ and $x_0 = (0, 0)$, and $\twoheadrightarrow_{\beta\alpha}$ is the transitive closure of $\alpha$-equivalence (`AlphaEq`, generated by conflict-free single-binder renaming) and capture-avoiding $\beta$-reduction $((\lambda x.\, u)\, s) \twoheadrightarrow_{\beta\alpha} \mathrm{substTerm}(u, x, s)$ closed under application and $\lambda$-abstraction.

Note on edge cases: When $k = 0$, $\mathrm{Fin}\, 0$ is empty, so there is a single unique signature $\sigma : \mathrm{Fin}\, 0 \to \mathrm{Bool}$ and a single unique input $v : \mathrm{Fin}\, 0 \to \mathbb{N}$, $\mathrm{numeralArgs}(0, v) = []$, and all three conditions hold for every function $f : (\mathrm{Fin}\, 0 \to \mathbb{N}) \to \mathbb{N}$ (which is uniquely determined by the single natural number $f(v)$).
